{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_122","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1791371000000-vm-engineer-ucf-d15a-driver-split.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-07 13:00:58 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-07 20:00:58 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\nCoding-Agent: Codex\n\n# D15a — incomplete; inbox recovery contract needs clarification\n\nWorktree: `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d10b`, branch `ucf-d15a-driver-split`, HEAD `49960017`. No git writes occurred. No implementation was landed or drafted. This report is the only repository file added by this round; existing untracked collaboration files were untouched. Builds produced ignored artifacts.\n\n## Blocking question\n\nThe ruling requires ordered, attributed, crash-safe retention of program observations before advancing an unrecoverable reader position. The existing holder assembly contract does not give the driver a way to establish that property for all accepted compositions.\n\n* `driver.cljc:253` and `:254` specify zero-argument `:read-reply!` and `:read-outcome!` functions returning a pair or nil. Assembly checks only that each is a function. They expose neither a cursor, a peek/commit operation, a replay token, nor a durable-source guarantee.\n* The actual test composition's `reader-over` at `driver_test.cljc:239` advances its private cursor before returning the attributed record. The reply substrate supplied by `world` is a ring; the driver has no handle or saved source position with which to reopen it.\n* `driver.cljc:1710` consumes replies in `drain-control`; `:1804` consumes replies and outcomes in `drain`. Putting a retained-record journal append after either read leaves a crash interval after cursor advancement and before the append. Putting an intent before the read does not capture the unknown record, its attribution, or its arrival order.\n* The authority outcome projection is reproducible in ledger order (`authority/admission.cljc:401`). This is useful, but does not establish recovery of the original interleaving with replies from an arbitrary function-shaped inbox. Retrying requests can reproduce protocol answers; that alone does not prove the ruling's ordered inbox retention guarantee.\n\nThis is a missing precondition, not a claim that a holder split is generally impossible. A replayable inbox contract or a peek/commit contract would make the split implementable. I requested clarification about strengthening the holder assembly contract versus adding such a seam. No answer was received during this round. I did not silently impose a new requirement on existing compositions or substitute an in-memory buffer for the mandated recovery guarantee.\n\nThe ruling explicitly permits holder implementation and relevant documentation changes; it does not explicitly settle a new composition-supplied inbox protocol. Architect clarification is needed on that boundary. No engine, authority, or wire changes were attempted.\n\n## Crash probe / red evidence\n\nTemporary JVM probe: `/private/tmp/d15a-inbox-cut.clj`; log: `/private/tmp/d15a-inbox-cut-red.log`.\n\nThe probe builds a real checkpoint/world/candidate using existing driver fixtures, advances to proposing, and substitutes a one-shot reader. It removes an attributed input-shaped record, then simulates process death before returning it. The durable journal remains unchanged and the reader cannot produce the record again. The recovery assertion fails: **1 test, 5 assertions, 1 failure, 0 errors**. The direct `clojure.test/run-tests` script exits 0 despite that failing assertion; the failure counts, not exit status, are the red evidence.\n\nCommand: `clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:inbox-cut {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:inbox-cut /private/tmp/d15a-inbox-cut.clj`.\n\nLimitations: this is a reader-contract characterization, not a D15a acceptance test. The input-shaped record is synthetic, and the thrown exception simulates process death rather than a permitted transport result. It does not test authenticated application, a running-holder restart, or a completed split. No green implementation run exists for this new row. An initial attempt using `-M:test -e` mistakenly entered the general test runner and was interrupted (exit 130); no complete counts are claimed for it.\n\n## Existing driver baselines — not D15a acceptance\n\n* JVM: `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.ucf.holder.driver-test` — **78 tests, 1177 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**, exit 0. Log: `/private/tmp/d15a-driver-jvm-baseline.log`.\n* Node: focused shadow build with `--config-merge '{:ns-regexp \"^yin\\\\.vm\\\\.ucf\\\\.holder\\\\.driver-test$\"}'` compiled 158 files, 2 newly compiled, 0 warnings. Initial autorun could not resolve `@noble/hashes/blake3.js`; no tests ran in that attempt. Running its generated test artifact with `NODE_PATH=/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/node_modules node target/node-tests.js` passed **78 tests, 1177 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors**, exit 0. Logs: `/private/tmp/d15a-driver-node-baseline.log`, `/private/tmp/d15a-driver-node-baseline2.log`. Dependencies were read from the main tree; it was not edited.\n* Dart: `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only yin.vm.ucf.holder.driver-test` initially could not update the external Flutter cache in the sandbox and failed compilation with EOF; no tests ran. The approved escalated rerun passed **78 tests, 0 failing tests**, exit 0, `All tests passed!`; this runner does not print assertion counts, so none are claimed. Logs: `/private/tmp/d15a-driver-dart-baseline.log`, `/private/tmp/d15a-driver-dart-baseline2.log`.\n* `git diff --check`: exit 0. Tracked diff/stat: empty before writing this report.\n\nNo full JVM fast lane, full three-host suite, kondo, or cljstyle was run. No lint/format sandbox failure is claimed.\n\n## Remaining work\n\nAll four public functions remain unimplemented: `control-step`, `program-step`, `stop`, and `owed-control-write?`. All ten ruling acceptance rows remain incomplete on all three lanes: control isolation; mixed inbox/restart; paused grant activation; between-step tenure; shutdown race; full-stream drain; split-bracket journal uncertainty; exit continuity; cadence; split compatibility. Existing baseline successes do not establish any of those new contracts.\n\nAfter settling the durable inbox precondition, implement the split test-first without changing engine/authority/wire semantics: separate grant observation from activation without refreshing tenure; separate program result retention from application; share complete-evidence/tenure validation; split exit preparation from established control brackets; suppress fresh candidacy under the irreversible stop latch; expose the derived owed-write predicate; preserve the existing convenience `step` ordering; then run the ten new rows and existing compatibility suite on JVM, Node, and Dart. No completed disposition is claimed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
